<div class="tab-content active" id="notes-2-7">

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Why Governments Intervene</h2>
    <p>Government intervention in markets can aim to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Earn government revenue</li>
      <li>Support firms or households on low incomes</li>
      <li>Influence the level of production or consumption</li>
      <li>Correct market failure</li>
      <li>Promote equity</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Price Controls</h2>
    <p>Price controls regulate prices above or below the market equilibrium.</p>

    <h3>Price ceiling (maximum price)</h3>
    <p>Set <strong>below</strong> the equilibrium to make goods more affordable. To be effective, it must be below the equilibrium price.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Consumers who can buy</td><td>Better off — pay a lower price</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumers who cannot buy</td><td>Worse off — shortage means some cannot get the good</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Producers</td><td>Worse off — receive lower price, sell less; revenue falls</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Society</td><td>Deadweight loss (DWL) — allocative inefficiency</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;">A <strong>black market</strong> can arise: firms or consumers illegally charge a price above the legal maximum to clear the shortage.</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/price-ceiling.svg" alt="A price ceiling set below equilibrium, creating a shortage where quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A binding ceiling below Pe: quantity supplied (Qs) falls short of quantity demanded (Qd), creating a shortage.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/price-ceiling-welfare.svg" alt="Welfare effects of a price ceiling showing consumer surplus, producer surplus and deadweight loss" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Welfare effects: output falls to Q&#8321;, producer surplus shrinks, and a deadweight loss (red) appears.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Price floor (minimum price)</h3>
    <p>Set <strong>above</strong> the equilibrium to increase the price producers receive. To be effective, it must be above the equilibrium price.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Producers who sell</td><td>Better off — receive a higher price</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumers</td><td>Worse off — pay a higher price and buy less</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unsold producers</td><td>Worse off — surplus means some producers cannot sell</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Society</td><td>Deadweight loss — allocative inefficiency; surplus may require government purchase</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/price-floor.svg" alt="A price floor set above equilibrium, creating a surplus where quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A binding floor above Pe: quantity supplied (Qs) exceeds quantity demanded (Qd), creating a surplus.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/price-floor-welfare.svg" alt="Welfare effects of a price floor showing consumer surplus, producer surplus and deadweight loss" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Welfare effects: output falls to Q&#8321;, consumer surplus shrinks, and a deadweight loss (red) appears.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Quantity Controls: Quotas</h2>
    <p>A <strong>quota</strong> caps the quantity that may be produced or sold. It is a control on
    <em>quantity</em> rather than on price, so it is drawn as a vertical line rather than a horizontal
    one. Examples include fishing quotas, production limits agreed by a cartel, and import quotas in
    international trade.</p>

    <p>A quota only bites if it is set <strong>below</strong> the free market quantity Qe. Once it does,
    the quantity traded falls to Q&#8321;, and because consumers are competing for fewer units, the price
    rises to P&#8321;, the price consumers are willing to pay for that restricted quantity.</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/quota-welfare.svg" alt="A quota set below the equilibrium quantity, showing consumer surplus, producer surplus and the deadweight loss triangle between the quota quantity and the equilibrium quantity" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A quota at Q&#8321;, below Qe. Price rises to P&#8321;, consumer surplus (blue) shrinks, producer surplus (amber) gains a transfer from consumers, and the red triangle between Q&#8321; and Qe is the deadweight loss.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Area</th><th>What it is after the quota</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Consumer surplus</strong></td>
          <td>Shrinks to the triangle under demand and above P&#8321;. Consumers lose twice: they pay
          a higher price on the units still traded, and they lose the units between Q&#8321; and Qe entirely.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Producer surplus</strong></td>
          <td>The area below P&#8321; and above the supply curve, up to Q&#8321;. Producers lose the surplus
          on the units they can no longer sell, but gain a transfer from consumers on the units they
          do sell, because P&#8321; is above Pe. The net effect can go either way. That transfer is
          called the <strong>quota rent</strong>, and whoever holds the quota captures it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Deadweight loss</strong></td>
          <td>The red triangle between demand and supply from Q&#8321; to Qe. Every unit in that range
          was worth more to a consumer than it cost a producer to make, so each one was a mutually
          beneficial trade. The quota prevents them, and that surplus is not transferred to anyone.
          It simply disappears.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout exam-tip">
      <div class="callout-title">Compare it with the price floor</div>
      <p>Look back at the price floor welfare diagram. The three areas are <em>identical</em>. A price
      floor and a quota that restrict output to the same Q&#8321; have exactly the same welfare effects.
      The difference is the instrument, not the outcome: a price floor sets the price and lets quantity
      fall out of it, while a quota sets the quantity and lets price fall out of it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="callout-title">Do not draw a quota as a horizontal line</div>
      <p>A quota restricts quantity, so it is a <strong>vertical</strong> line at Q&#8321;. Drawing it
      horizontally turns it into a price control and loses the marks. The higher price P&#8321; is a
      <em>consequence</em> of the quota, read off the demand curve at Q&#8321;, not something the
      government set.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Indirect Taxes</h2>
    <p>An <strong>indirect tax</strong> is a tax imposed on expenditure on goods and services. "Indirect" because consumers pay the producer, and the producer pays the government — the producer is the key actor in collection and payment.</p>

    <h3>Types</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Specific tax:</strong> fixed amount per unit (e.g. $2 per packet of cigarettes)</li>
      <li><strong>Ad valorem tax:</strong> a percentage of the selling price (e.g. 20% VAT)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why governments use indirect taxes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Raise government revenue</li>
      <li>Reduce consumption of demerit goods or goods with negative externalities</li>
      <li>Broaden the tax base</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Effect on the market</h3>
    <p>The tax is paid by producers → it increases costs → supply shifts left (decreases). The vertical distance between the original and new supply curves equals the tax per unit. New equilibrium: higher price for consumers, lower price received by producers, lower quantity.</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/indirect-tax.svg" alt="A specific tax shifting supply up to S plus tax, with consumer price Pc, producer price Pp, tax revenue and deadweight loss" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The tax shifts supply to S + tax. Consumers pay Pc, producers keep Pp; the box is government revenue and the red triangle is the deadweight loss.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Effects on stakeholders</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Consumers</td><td>Pay a higher price (Pc); buy less</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Producers</td><td>Receive a lower price (Pp = Pc − tax per unit); sell less; revenue falls</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Government</td><td>Gains tax revenue = tax per unit × quantity sold (Qt)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Workers</td><td>May see increased unemployment as quantity produced falls</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Society</td><td>Deadweight loss — small triangle of lost social surplus</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Tax incidence</h3>
    <p>Tax incidence refers to who actually bears the burden of the tax. This depends on PED:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Inelastic demand:</strong> consumers bear more of the tax — they cannot easily reduce quantity demanded, so producers can pass most of the tax on as higher prices</li>
      <li><strong>Elastic demand:</strong> producers bear more of the tax — if they raise prices, Qd falls sharply, so producers absorb more of the cost</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="graph-figure wide">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/tax-incidence-elasticity.svg" alt="Two panels showing the tax burden falling mostly on consumers when demand is inelastic and mostly on producers when demand is elastic" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The same tax: with inelastic demand consumers bear most of the burden; with elastic demand producers bear most.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Subsidies</h2>
    <p>A <strong>subsidy</strong> is a payment from the government to producers, lowering their costs and shifting supply right (down) to S + subsidy. Producers receive a higher price (Pp), consumers pay a lower price (Pc), and quantity rises.</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/subsidy.svg" alt="A subsidy shifting supply down to S plus subsidy, with producer price Pp, consumer price Pc, total subsidy cost and deadweight loss" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The subsidy shifts supply to S + subsidy. Producers receive Pp, consumers pay Pc; the box is the total cost to government and the red triangle is the deadweight loss from overprovision.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="callout remember">
      <div class="callout-title">Materials to come</div>
      <p>Fuller notes on subsidies — their effects on consumers, producers, government spending, and efficiency — will be added here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

